/* book exam traps: Saying high voltage is dangerous and stopping there. A 230 V source is harmless through 100 kΩ and lethal through 1 kΩ; the current decides. | Writing micro-shock limits in milliamps. The intracardiac danger level is tens to about a hundred microamps, a thousand to several thousand times smaller than the 100 mA macro-shock level (20 µA is 5000 times smaller). | Mixing up let-go and fibrillation. Let-go is 10–15 mA; fibrillation is near 100 mA for a one-second shock. | Ignoring duration and timing. A short pulse, or a pulse outside the T wave, is far less likely to fibrillate than a one-second shock. | Treating body resistance as one fixed number. It changes tenfold between dry and wet skin and drops again once a conductor is inside the body. */
